PATCH 610: unaff_CS is the running code segment (push-cs), = this cluster's CS -- board:0010/0012.

The decompile reads `unaff_CS` (CS register, unaffected in the function) uninitialised at 52 sites and
passes it on -- overwhelmingly the NovaLogic `push cs ; call/lcall` idiom (CLAUDE.md: "push cs SAVE the
running code segment").  CS inside a Ghidra FUN_SEG_off function IS that cluster's segment (the name
encodes it): FUN_1000_* -> 0x1000, FUN_0000_*/app_entry/caseD_* -> 0x0000, FUN_0f69_* -> 0x0f69.  Ghidra
left it an uninitialised C local because CS context is not set (board:0010, the pipeline gap).

Reading an uninitialised local is UNDEFINED BEHAVIOUR, and it is the sole cause of the in-mission
native != wasm divergence (board:0012): gcc -O0 (native) reads deterministic stack garbage, gcc -O2
(the wasm build's level, and clang/emcc) treats it as undefined and evaluates it differently, so the two
targets diverge mid-mission (first at INDIA1 t=295 -> the framebuffer differs by 70 KB at AZER1 t=2000).
Proven by isolation: -O0 vs -O2 diverge at the same tick (same platform, so it is UB not a platform gap);
zeroing ONLY the unaff_CS/unaff_ES declarations converges both; and the single unaff_CS in FUN_1000_a7e0
(`push cs ; call 5591`, 1000:a7e0) is the exact site for INDIA1 -- with `= 0x1000` there, and with the
whole class resolved to the cluster CS here, the per-tick DGROUP SIMHASH is byte-identical native==wasm
to resolution across AZER1/CYPRUS1/INDIA1/SAUDI1/SYRIA1.  The value is the asm-correct one (the segment
the original CPU pushes), not merely deterministic, so it is congruent with the original, not a guard or
approximation.
